zed) vs. Colony (decentralized)"],"source_memory_refs":["SCR_001"],"transfer_claims":["The trade-off between specialized internal regulation and diverse external survival applies to network architecture."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are more resilient to 'black swan' failures but more vulnerable to 'slow-burn' systemic shifts than centralized services.","counterconditions":["The decentralized network implements a robust, automated consensus-based recovery mechanism.","The centralized service's coordination logic is the primary source of its correlated failures."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Immune System vs. Colony' mechanism: A centralized service's redundancy acts as a powerful, singular immune system capable of rapid, global rollback (high recovery speed), but a single failure in the coordination logic can lead to an instantaneous 'Flash Crash'. A decentralized network acts as a colony; it lacks a global kill-switch (slower propagation of some failures), but it is susceptible to environmental shifts (e.g., a change in a global protocol) that a centralized entity could have buffered or regulated internally.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between independent and correlated failures can be identified by the 'Latency-Correlation Signature' of the failure event.","counterconditions":["The 'shared cause' is so subtle that its trigger is indistinguishable from background noise.","Independent operators react to the same external event in identical ways, mimicking a shared internal cause."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Temporal-Spatial Signature' mechanism: Truly independent failures occur as a Poisson process—stochastically distributed across time and space. Correlated failures, even those appearing independent, exhibit 'latent synchronization': they cluster around a specific trigger event (e.g., a leap second, a specific API update, or a solar flare) with a characteristic propagation delay that mirrors the network's topology rather than random chance.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are more resilient to correlated failure only if the 'diversity index' of their implementations exceeds the 'redundancy coefficient' of the centralized alternative.","counterconditions":["Centralized services implement 'N-version programming' (multiple independent implementations of the same spec).","The cost of maintaining high heterogeneity in a decentralized network creates new, unique failure modes (e.g., interoperability collapse)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Heterogeneity Threshold' mechanism: Redundancy in a centralized system is often just duplication (same code, different server). Resilience in a decentralized system comes from diversity (different code, different server). If the decentralized network is merely a collection of clones, it is less resilient. Only when the variety of failure modes (entropy) is high enough to ensure that no single trigger can affect a majority of nodes does the decentralized model win.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we formally define the 'Diversity Index' mentioned in SCR_005 to create a mathematical proof for the resilience threshold?","related_refs":["SCR_005","SCR_006"]},{"question":"Is the 'Flash Crash' of a centralized system actually a form of 'hyper-resilience' if the recovery is truly instantaneous?","related_refs":["SCR_003"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}